 <LineBreak children='\n'>,
 <RawText children='|13| False (egg contributes most cytoplasm) |'>,
 <LineBreak children='\n'>,
 <RawText children='|14| False (only angiosperms) |'>,
 <LineBreak children='\n'>,
 <RawText children='|15| False (nutrients from yolk, not placenta) |'>,
 <LineBreak children='\n'>,
 <RawText children=('|16| Binary fission = a single cell divides into two identical daughter '
 'cells; example: ')>,
 <Emphasis children=[<RawText children='Escherichia coli'>]>,
 <RawText children='. |'>,
 <LineBreak children='\n'>,
 <RawText children=('|17| The pollen tube grows from the pollen grain down the style, delivering '
 'two sperm cells to the ovule; one fertilizes the egg, the other fuses with '
 'polar nuclei to form endosperm. |')>,
 <LineBreak children='\n'>,
 <RawText children=('|18| Advantages: (i) Genetic diversity, (ii) Adaptability to changing '
 'environments, (iii) Ability to purge deleterious mutations. Disadvantages: '
 '(i) Requires finding a mate, (ii) Higher energetic cost (gamete production, '
 'courtship, parental care). |')>,
 <LineBreak children='\n'>,
 <RawText children=('|19| After ovulation, the ruptured follicle becomes the corpus luteum, which '
 'secretes progesterone (dominant) and some estrogen; progesterone prepares '
 'the endometrium for possible implantation and inhibits further ovulation. |')>,
 <LineBreak children='\n'>,
 <RawText children='|20| '>,
 <StrongEmphasis children=[<RawText children='External fertilization'>]>,
 <RawText children=(': occurs in water; gametes released simultaneously; high numbers of eggs '
 'needed; low parental care; vulnerable to dilution, temperature, predation. ')>,
 <StrongEmphasis children=[<RawText children='Internal fertilization'>]>,
 <RawText children=(': occurs inside female body; fewer gametes needed; higher chance of '
 'fertilization; often accompanied by parental care; allows reproduction in '
 'terrestrial habitats. |')>,
 <LineBreak children='\n'>,
 <RawText children='|Diagram| (teacher’s grading based on correct labels). |'>]>,
 <BlankLine children=[]>,
 <ThematicBreak children=[]>,
 <BlankLine children=[]>,
 <Heading children=[<RawText children='Summary'>]>,
 <BlankLine children=[]>,
 <Paragraph children=[<RawText children='Reproduction unites two fundamental biological themes: '>,
 <StrongEmphasis children=[<RawText children='continuity of life'>]>,
 <RawText children=' and '>,
 <StrongEmphasis children=[<RawText children='generation of diversity'>]>,
 <RawText children=('. Asexual reproduction offers speed and simplicity but limits genetic '
 'variation, while sexual reproduction, though energetically demanding, '
 'creates the raw material for evolution. In plants, the intricate dance of '
 'pollination, double fertilization, and seed development illustrates how '
 'sexual reproduction can be tightly linked to ecological interactions. '
 'Animals display a spectrum of reproductive strategies—from external '
 'fertilization in aquatic habitats to complex viviparity and parental care in '
 'mammals. Understanding the human reproductive system provides a concrete '
 'example of these principles and highlights the importance of hormonal '
 'regulation and health considerations.  ')>]>,
 <BlankLine children=[]>,
 <Paragraph children=[<RawText children=('The test outlined above evaluates students’ grasp of definitions, '
 'mechanisms, and evolutionary implications, preparing them for further study '
 'in genetics, ecology, and human biology. Good luck!')>]>]>
